<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/"><channel><title>Pfc on metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/linked_project/pfc/</link><description>Research Engineer &amp;amp; Computer Scientist - Machine Learning, Statistical Computing, Open Source Development</description><language>en-us</language><managingEditor>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</managingEditor><webMaster>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</webMaster><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 08:44:23 -0500</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://queelius.github.io/linked_project/pfc/" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><image><url>https://queelius.github.io/images/og-image.jpg</url><title>metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/</link></image><item><title>Synthesis: Codecs as Structure</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-synthesis-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Fri, 15 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-synthesis-wire-formats/</guid><description>The series closes by restating the codes-as-priors thesis across all twelve instances and connecting the wire-format side to the Stepanov type-algebra side.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-synthesis-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>synthesis</category><category>algebra</category></item><item><title>Bits Follow Types</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-codecs-functors-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Thu, 23 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-codecs-functors-stepanov/</guid><description>Codecs are not ad-hoc bit formats. They are constructions on the algebraic structure of types.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-codecs-functors-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>codecs</category><category>information-theory</category><category>category-theory</category><category>functors</category></item><item><title>When Lists Become Bits</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-prefix-free-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Thu, 23 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-prefix-free-stepanov/</guid><description>Prefix-freeness is the property that lifts the free-monoid construction into bit space.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-prefix-free-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>codecs</category><category>information-theory</category><category>category-theory</category><category>monoids</category></item><item><title>RoaringBitmap</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-roaring-bitmap-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 07 Dec 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-roaring-bitmap-wire-formats/</guid><description>A hybrid compressed bitmap that picks the optimal sub-representation (array, bitmap, or run-length) per 64K-integer chunk based on density. No single prior dominates: Roaring commits to none and adapts per chunk.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-roaring-bitmap-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>data-structures</category><category>compressed-bitmaps</category><category>roaring</category><category>polyalgorithm</category></item><item><title>Succinct Bit Vectors and Rank/Select</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-06-succinct-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 22 Jun 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-06-succinct-wire-formats/</guid><description>A bit vector with O(1) rank and O(log n) select using only n + o(n) bits of space. The auxiliary index is asymptotically negligible while enabling constant-time queries.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-06-succinct-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>data-structures</category><category>succinct</category><category>rank-select</category></item><item><title>Arithmetic Coding</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-arithmetic-coding-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 12 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-arithmetic-coding-wire-formats/</guid><description>Arithmetic coding closes the gap between Huffman&amp;#39;s per-symbol integer lengths and true entropy. A single number in the unit interval encodes an entire sequence; 32-bit integer arithmetic makes it practical.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-arithmetic-coding-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>entropy</category><category>arithmetic-coding</category><category>range-coding</category></item><item><title>Huffman Coding</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-08-huffman-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 04 Aug 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-08-huffman-wire-formats/</guid><description>Given a finite distribution, Huffman&amp;#39;s algorithm builds the prefix-free code with minimum expected length. It is the first entropy-optimal code in this series.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-08-huffman-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>huffman</category><category>entropy-optimal</category></item><item><title>PFC: Zero-Copy Data Compression Through Prefix-Free Codecs</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-06-pfc/</link><pubDate>Mon, 10 Jun 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-06-pfc/</guid><description>A header-only C++20 library that achieves 3-10x compression with zero marshaling overhead using prefix-free codes and Stepanov-style generic programming.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-06-pfc/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>compression</category><category>prefix-free-codes</category><category>zero-copy</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>STL</category><category>template-metaprogramming</category><category>Elias-codes</category></item><item><title>VByte / Varint</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-02-vbyte-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 25 Feb 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-02-vbyte-wire-formats/</guid><description>VByte trades bit-level precision for byte-alignment, and that trade wins in practice. Most production columnar databases and network protocols use VByte for integer encoding.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-02-vbyte-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>vbyte</category><category>varint</category><category>byte-aligned</category></item><item><title>Rice / Golomb</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-09-rice-golomb-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 17 Sep 2023 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-09-rice-golomb-wire-formats/</guid><description>Rice and Golomb codes are parametric: a single parameter k (or m) tunes the code to a specific geometric distribution. Choosing k is choosing your prior precisely.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-09-rice-golomb-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>rice</category><category>golomb</category><category>geometric-distribution</category></item><item><title>Fibonacci Coding</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-04-fibonacci-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 23 Apr 2023 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-04-fibonacci-wire-formats/</guid><description>Fibonacci coding uses Zeckendorf&amp;#39;s representation to produce self-synchronizing codewords. Every codeword ends in two consecutive ones; a single bit flip corrupts at most two codewords.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-04-fibonacci-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>fibonacci</category><category>zeckendorf</category></item><item><title>Elias Delta and Omega</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-11-elias-delta-omega-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 13 Nov 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-11-elias-delta-omega-wire-formats/</guid><description>Elias delta and omega extend Elias gamma by recursively encoding the length prefix. Each step yields shorter codewords for large integers at a small constant cost for small ones.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-11-elias-delta-omega-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>elias-delta</category><category>elias-omega</category></item><item><title>Unary and Elias Gamma</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-06-elias-gamma-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 19 Jun 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-06-elias-gamma-wire-formats/</guid><description>Unary and Elias gamma are the two simplest universal codes. Unary encodes n in n bits; gamma in 2 log2(n)+1 bits. Each implies a different prior over the integers.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-06-elias-gamma-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>unary</category><category>elias-gamma</category></item><item><title>Universal Codes as Priors</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-01-priors-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sat, 15 Jan 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-01-priors-wire-formats/</guid><description>Every prefix-free code is a hypothesis about the source. The codeword lengths determine an implicit probability distribution; the code is optimal when that prior matches the true source.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-01-priors-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>Shannon</category></item><item><title>McMillan&amp;#39;s Converse</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-09-mcmillan-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 13 Sep 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-09-mcmillan-wire-formats/</guid><description>Any length vector satisfying Kraft has a prefix-free code. Here is the construction.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-09-mcmillan-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>constructive-proof</category></item><item><title>Kraft&amp;#39;s Inequality</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-03-kraft-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 22 Mar 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-03-kraft-wire-formats/</guid><description>Which codeword-length vectors are achievable by prefix-free codes? Kraft&amp;#39;s inequality is the answer.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-03-kraft-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Pfc</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>combinatorics</category></item></channel></rss>